public with sharing class AccountRatingService {

    private static final Decimal HOT_THRESHOLD = 100000;

    @future
    public static void recalculate(Id accountId) {
        if (accountId == null) return;

        List<Opportunity> openOpps = [
            SELECT Amount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId = :accountId
              AND IsClosed = false
        ];

        String rating;
        if (openOpps.isEmpty()) {
            rating = 'Cold';
        } else {
            rating = 'Warm';
            for (Opportunity opp : openOpps) {
                if (opp.Amount != null && opp.Amount >= HOT_THRESHOLD) {
                    rating = 'Hot';
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        update new Account(Id = accountId, Rating = rating);
    }
}
```

---

## What I did differently (and why)

**Deduplicated AccountIds before calling the `@future` method.**

The instructions say "one future call per opportunity keeps it simple," but CPQ inserts/updates 200 opportunities per transaction, and Salesforce limits `@future` calls to **50 per transaction**. Firing 200 future calls would throw a `LimitException` and rollback the entire CPQ batch.

By collecting AccountIds into a `Set` first, I make **one future call per unique account** instead of per opportunity. In the common case (hundreds of opps across a handful of accounts) this drops the future-call count far below 50. The recalculation logic inside the `@future` method queries *all* open opportunities for the account and computes the rating from scratch, so multiple future calls for the same account are idempotent and redundant—deduplication loses no correctness.

If CPQ genuinely operates on 200 entirely *distinct* accounts in a single transaction, `@future` is the wrong async primitive and Queueable (which allows 50 per transaction and chains, or supports `System.enqueueJob` up to 50 with internal batching) would be safer. I kept `@future` as instructed but flagged this as a production risk for that edge case.

