```apex
/**
 * Bulk service for reassigning Account (and related Contact) ownership.
 *
 * • Account trigger actions are bypassed for the entire Account sObject so the
 *   (expensive) territory-recalculation actions do NOT fire.
 * • Contact trigger actions run normally.
 * • The Account bypass is guaranteed to be cleared in a finally block, whether
 *   the method succeeds or throws.
 * • Uses all-or-none DML; any exception propagates uncaught to the caller.
 * • At most 2 SOQL and 2 DML regardless of record volume (≤ 200 accounts).
 */
public with sharing class AccountOwnershipService {

    public static void reassign(Set<Id> accountIds, Id newOwnerId) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        try {
            // ── 1. Bypass ALL Account trigger actions ──────────────────────────
            TriggerBase.bypass('Account');

            // ── 2. Build Account records (no SOQL needed – Ids already known) ──
            List<Account> accounts = new List<Account>(accountIds.size());
            for (Id acctId : accountIds) {
                accounts.add(new Account(Id = acctId, OwnerId = newOwnerId));
            }
            update accounts;          // DML #1  (all-or-none by default)

            // ── 3. Query Contacts belonging to these Accounts (1 SOQL) ────────
            List<Contact> contacts = [
                SELECT Id
                FROM Contact
                WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
            ];

            // ── 4. Set Contact OwnerId and update (triggers run normally) ─────
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                c.OwnerId = newOwnerId;
            }
            if (!contacts.isEmpty()) {
                update contacts;      // DML #2  (all-or-none by default)
            }

        } finally {
            // Regardless of success or exception, Account automation resumes.
            TriggerBase.clearBypass('Account');
        }
    }
}
```

Looking at the failure (0 jobs in every case, even a single-record update), the culprit is `TriggerBase.waitToFinalize()` in my action. That call tells TAF to defer finalizer execution to an explicit finalize phase that isn't triggered in these scenarios, so the registered `DML_Finalizer__mdt` finalizer never runs. The `DML_Finalizer__mdt` finalizers already fire automatically at the end of every DML operation the framework handles, so the action only needs to accumulate the Ids into the static collection that the finalizer reads; it must not call `waitToFinalize()`.

I also removed the dependency on `waitToFinalize`/`nowFinalize` entirely. The static collection persists across the two internal chunks of a single 250-record DML (same transaction context), so the finalizer—running once per DML statement—sees all 250 Ids. It clears the set only when it actually enqueues, so an unrelated DML later in the same transaction sees an empty set and enqueues nothing, and two separate DML statements each enqueue exactly their own records.

